# The .dag Format — Formal Specification v1.0

The `.dag` file format is a compiled, machine-optimized representation of a spec genome. It is the output of the spec engine — a Directed Acyclic Graph encoded as a JSON document.

A `.dag` file IS the graph. Nodes are entities. Edges are relationships. Properties are typed values. No prose. No markdown. Pure structure.

## Grammar

```
dag-file ::= {
  "version": "1.0",
  "project": string,
  "compiled_at": ISO8601,
  "score": 0-100,
  "nodes": [node, ...],
  "edges": [edge, ...],
  "predictions": [prediction, ...],
  "vectors": [vector, ...]
}

node ::= {
  "id": string,
  "name": string,
  "type": node-type,
  "description": string,
  "properties": { prop-name: property-def, ... },
  "states": [string, ...],
  "lifecycle": [string, ...],
  "source_file": string,
  "source_line": number
}

edge ::= {
  "source": string,
  "target": string,
  "verb": verb-name,
  "cardinality": "1:1" | "1:N" | "N:1" | "N:M",
  "required": boolean,
  "cascade": "none" | "delete" | "nullify" | "restrict",
  "invariants": string
}

prediction ::= {
  "id": string,
  "statement": string,
  "trigger": string | null,
  "timeframe": string | null,
  "confidence": 0.0-1.0,
  "measurement": string,
  "actual": "pending" | "correct" | "wrong" | "partially_correct",
  "resolved_at": ISO8601 | null
}

vector ::= {
  "node_id": string,
  "content": string,
  "embedding": [number, ...],
  "model": "all-MiniLM-L6-v2" | "text-embedding-3-small"
}
```

## Pipeline

```
.dog (human writes)        .dag (machine compiles)
───────────────            ───────────────
Markdown + YAML            Pure JSON
Human-readable             Machine-parseable
Prose for context          No prose — just graph
Source of truth            Compiled artifact
Stored in git              Stored in SQLite
Free, open source          Paid, enterprise
Dogfood format             Analytics format
```

## Relationship

- `.dog` is the INPUT format. Humans write it. AI reads it at build time.
- `.dag` is the OUTPUT format. The engine compiles `.dog` into `.dag`. AI traverses it at runtime.

```
SPEC.dog ──→ spec parse ──→ AST ──→ spec compile ──→ project.dag
                                            ↓
                                     SQLite / Postgres
                                            ↓
                                     AI traverses graph
```

## API

```
GET  /projects/:id/dag          → project.dag (full graph)
GET  /projects/:id/dag/nodes    → all nodes
GET  /projects/:id/dag/edges    → all edges
GET  /projects/:id/dag/node/:id → single node + connected edges
POST /projects/:id/compile      → recompile .dog → .dag
```

## AI Agent Instructions

When an AI agent receives a `.dag` file:

1. Parse as JSON
2. Build a graph: nodes as vertices, edges as directed connections
3. Traverse for queries: BFS for hop depth, DFS for paths
4. Compute predictions: confidence × historical_accuracy = expected_outcome
5. Flag nodes with no description as unembeddable
6. Flag edges referencing unknown nodes as broken

The `.dag` format is NOT meant for human editing. It is the compiled output of the spec engine.
